About The Integration & Corneal Oxygenation^^^
Because the cornea lacks blood vessels and requires direct oxygen diffusion from the air,
a multi-layer solid lens risks causing hypoxia and stromal edema. This is solved via an active micro-perfusion network:
Nanoporous Scaffolding: Continuous, vertical nano-channels ($20–50\text{ nm}$ in diameter) are integrated across all three structural layers via
ion-track methodology or self-assembled peptide pores.Selective Ion/Gas Flux: The nano-channels are lined with hydrophilic zwitterionic peptide sequences that permit
the unhindered diffusion of dissolved oxygen ($O_2$) and water molecules while physically blocking larger tear proteins, enzymes (lysozymes),
and bacteria.Tear-Pumping Action: The natural vertical motion of the eyelid during a blink acts as a micro-piston,
actively forcing fresh tear fluid through the nanopores to maintain continuous corneal respiration.
